<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <base href="/" />
    <ResourcePreloader />
    <link rel="icon" href="favicon.ico" sizes="any" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/png" href="@Assets["img/logo_red_gear.png"]" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["app.css"]" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["MT.Uptime.Web.styles.css"]" />
    @* <ImportMap /> is deliberately absent.
       It renders an inline <script type="importmap">, which CSP governs under script-src — so keeping it
       would force 'unsafe-inline' there and give up the one directive that actually matters, since
       'unsafe-inline' is what turns any HTML injection into script execution.
       It is safe to drop *because this app has no dynamic module imports*: its only three scripts are
       loaded by explicit fingerprinted src (blazor.web.js, js/copy-field.js and js/nav.js), and
       ReconnectModal.razor.js is referenced the same way rather than imported.
       IF YOU ADD a collocated `.razor.js` loaded the usual way —
           await JS.InvokeAsync<IJSObjectReference>("import", "./Components/Thing.razor.js")
       — the specifier will not resolve to its fingerprinted filename without the import map. Restore
       <ImportMap /> and give it a per-request nonce that the CSP in Program.cs also emits; do not simply
       add 'unsafe-inline'. *@
    <title>MT-Uptime</title>
    <HeadOutlet />
</head>

<body>
    <Routes />
    <ReconnectModal />
    <script src="@Assets["_framework/blazor.web.js"]"></script>
    <script src="@Assets["js/copy-field.js"]"></script>
    <script src="@Assets["js/nav.js"]"></script>
</body>

</html>
